{"role":"assistant","content":"","tool_calls":[{"id":"call_00_IlPXxZJjylxY6eqxRjTU9735","type":"function","function":{"name":"run_skill","arguments":"{\"arguments\": \"Read 3-4 of the ALH (文科) paper extracts from C:\\\\Users\\\\27063\\\\Desktop\\\\工具箱\\\\论文降低aigc分析文献库\\\\extracted\\\\ALH\\\\ and identify the actual writing style patterns of real human Chinese academic writers. Focus on:\\n1. How do they start paragraphs? (not \\\"首先/其次\\\", not template patterns)\\n2. What transition words/phrases do real Chinese scholars actually use?\\n3. How long are their typical paragraphs? What's the internal structure?\\n4. How do they express uncertainty vs certainty?\\n5. What's the typical sentence rhythm? Do they use short punchy sentences mid-paragraph?\\n6. What's the typical paragraph length and structure?\\n7. How do they cite and reference other works naturally?\\n8. What does their \\\"学术口吻\\\" actually sound like - the real rhythm, the real flow?\\n\\nRead these specific files for maximum variety:\\n- 关于人工智能的价值反思_孙伟平.txt (philosophy, shorter)\\n- 企业ESG表现与创新——来自A股上市公司的证据_方先明.txt (empirical economics)\\n- 儒家传统与企业创新_文化的力量_徐细雄.txt (cultural economics)\\n- 人工智能与人的\\\"新异化\\\"_孙伟平.txt (philosophy)\\n\\nExtract concrete writing patterns, not just statistics. Show before/after style comparisons.\", \"name\": \"explore\"}"}}],"reasoning_content":"The user wants me to write a new version of the paper, but this time I should learn from the human writing features in the reference papers (the 25 CNKI papers I extracted earlier). Instead of applying anti-AIGC rules after the fact, I should write the paper FROM SCRATCH mimicking the actual writing patterns found in real human-written academic papers.\n\nLet me think about what this means. I have 21 ALH (文科) papers as references. I should:\n1. Analyze their actual writing patterns - not just the statistical metrics (CoV, sentence length), but the actual rhetorical patterns, how they transition between ideas, how they structure arguments\n2. Write the new paper imitating those patterns\n3. The key insight: instead of writing \"normally\" and then applying anti-AIGC tricks, I should write IN THE STYLE OF the real papers from the start\n\nLet me re-read the extracted papers to understand the actual human writing style - the flow, the transitions, the paragraph structures, the sentence patterns that real Chinese humanities scholars use.\n\nLet me look at some of the actual paper content to understand the human writing pattern."}
