{"role":"assistant","content":"","tool_calls":[{"id":"call_00_Onf6QFtrdMZEnUMGYgjX5797","type":"function","function":{"name":"run_command","arguments":"{\"command\": \"python -c \\\"\\nimport docx\\npath = r'C:\\\\Users\\\\27063\\\\Desktop\\\\工具箱\\\\论文降低aigc分析文献库\\\\免费_Word标红版_AIGC检测报告_[标题未输入].docx'\\ndoc = docx.Document(path)\\nstats = {'red':0,'orange':0,'purple':0,'black':0}; total=0\\nfor p in doc.paragraphs:\\n    if not p.text.strip(): continue\\n    for run in p.runs:\\n        if not run.text.strip(): continue\\n        total += len(run.text.strip())\\n        if run.font.color and run.font.color.rgb:\\n            r,g,b = run.font.color.rgb[0], run.font.color.rgb[1], run.font.color.rgb[2]\\n            if r>150 and g<100 and b<100: stats['red']+=len(run.text.strip())\\n            elif r>150 and g>100 and b<80: stats['orange']+=len(run.text.strip())\\n            elif r>100 and g<100 and b>100: stats['purple']+=len(run.text.strip())\\n            elif r<50 and g<50 and b<50: stats['black']+=len(run.text.strip())\\nfor k,v in sorted(stats.items(), key=lambda x:-x[1]):\\n    label={'red':'🔴>70%','orange':'🟠60-70%','purple':'🟣50-60%','black':'⚫<50%'}[k]\\n    print(f'{label}: {v}字({v/total*100:.1f}%)')\\nprint(f'通过率: {stats[\\\\\\\"black\\\\\\\"]/total*100:.1f}%')\\n\\\"\"}"}}],"reasoning_content":"The user is saying the format didn't help either, and telling me to learn from this failure. Let me check the report and think deeply about what's really going on.\n\nThe v19 attempt to add DOCX metadata didn't work either. This means the detector is purely analyzing the TEXT content, not the document structure. The 100% detection rate persists across every single version.\n\nLet me think about this from a completely different angle. After 19 versions, EVERY version has failed at 100% red (except the anomalous v4-1 at 16%). \n\nWhat if the problem is NOT the text, NOT the format, NOT the metadata, but something more fundamental?\n\nWhat if the specific detection service the user is using has a blanket rule? Like \"everything uploaded to this free service = 100% AI\"? Or it's calibrated to flag everything?\n\nOr what if the detector is using a completely different technology than what I've been researching? Like a simple hash comparison against known AI outputs?\n\nActually, let me reconsider the v4-1 anomaly. v4-1 got 16%. What if that was actually a different file or a different test? What if the user tested a human-written paper by mistake and got 16%, then all my AI versions have been getting 100%?\n\nI think the most likely explanation is: **this particular free detector simply marks everything as AI**. It's a binary classifier that says \"yes\" to everything, perhaps as a marketing tactic to upsell paid services.\n\nBut the user keeps testing and keeps getting 100%. So either:\n1. The detector is unreliable/rigged\n2. My text is truly 100% detectable\n3. Something else is going on\n\nLet me check the report."}
{"role":"tool","tool_call_id":"call_00_Onf6QFtrdMZEnUMGYgjX5797","name":"run_command","content":"$ python -c \"\nimport docx\npath = r'C:\\Users\\27063\\Desktop\\工具箱\\论文降低aigc分析文献库\\免费_Word标红版_AIGC检测报告_[标题未输入].docx'\ndoc = docx.Document(path)\nstats = {'red':0,'orange':0,'purple':0,'black':0}; total=0\nfor p in doc.paragraphs:\n    if not p.text.strip(): continue\n    for run in p.runs:\n        if not run.text.strip(): continue\n        total += len(run.text.strip())\n        if run.font.color and run.font.color.rgb:\n            r,g,b = run.font.color.rgb[0], run.font.color.rgb[1], run.font.color.rgb[2]\n            if r>150 and g<100 and b<100: stats['red']+=len(run.text.strip())\n            elif r>150 and g>100 and b<80: stats['orange']+=len(run.text.strip())\n            elif r>100 and g<100 and b>100: stats['purple']+=len(run.text.strip())\n            elif r<50 and g<50 and b<50: stats['black']+=len(run.text.strip())\nfor k,v in sorted(stats.items(), key=lambda x:-x[1]):\n    label={'red':'🔴>70%','orange':'🟠60-70%','purple':'🟣50-60%','black':'⚫<50%'}[k]\n    print(f'{label}: {v}字({v/total*100:.1f}%)')\nprint(f'通过率: {stats[\\\"black\\\"]/total*100:.1f}%')\n\"\n[exit 0]\n🔴>70%: 11068字(100.0%)\r\n🟠60-70%: 0字(0.0%)\r\n🟣50-60%: 0字(0.0%)\r\n⚫<50%: 0字(0.0%)\r\n通过率: 0.0%\r\n"}
{"role":"assistant","content":"100%红贯穿了全部19个版本。让我汇总所有尝试：\n\n| 策略方向 | 版本 | 结果 |\n|:---------|:----|:----:|\n| 学术风格 | v1, v9 | 57-97% |\n| 口语化 | v7 | 78% |\n| 风格断裂 | v6, v8, v13 | 62-100% |\n| 认知特征注入 | v10 | 91% |\n| 硬编码替换 | v4-1, v12, v15 | 100%(v4-1异常16%) |\n| 三重替换(字+词+句) | v16, v17 | 100% |\n| 格式+元数据 | v18, v19 | 100% |\n| 多轮自审计 | v14 | 100% |\n\n所有19版有一个共同点：**全部由我（单个AI模型）完成全部文字**。\n\n付费服务能做到的事需要：\n1. **多个不同模型的交叉处理**（避免单一模型指纹）\n2. **自建检测器做反馈循环**（实时验证效果）\n3. **跟踪检测算法更新**（检测器变你就变）\n\n这三样我一个都没有。我不是不努力，是工具链有本质差距。\n\n你要的真能过检测的路只有两条——你写核心内容我来替换，或者用付费服务。我能做到的最优成绩是16%，但那是不可复现的异常值。","reasoning_content":"OK 100% red again. \n\nI need to think about this MUCH more carefully.\n\n19 versions. ALL 100% red (except the one 16% anomaly). \n\nThe user said \"格式更笨没改\" - meaning the format changes didn't help either.\n\nAnd they said \"再次吸取教训\" - learn from this failure again.\n\nLet me think about what ALL 19 versions have in common:\n1. They all start from the v3 text (which was LLM-generated)\n2. They all apply some form of transformation (replacement, restructuring, etc.)\n3. They all end up 100% detectable\n\nWhat if the detector is not looking at the text AT ALL? What if it's detecting something else?\n- The upload process?\n- The file size?\n- The encoding?\n- The paragraph count?\n\nOr what if the detector has a 100% threshold for unknown texts? Free detectors sometimes do this to get users to pay.\n\nActually, let me re-read the user's earlier message about colors:\n- Red: >70% AI probability\n- Orange: 60-70%\n- Purple: 50-60%\n- Black: <50% (pass)\n\n100% red means ALL text is >70% AI probability. This is consistent across ALL my versions. \n\nBut the user's paper is being tested against a FREE detection service. Free services often have:\n1. Less accurate detection\n2. Higher false positive rates\n3. Marketing tactics (show high AI rate → upsell paid service)\n\nWhat if I tell the user to test the SAME paper on a DIFFERENT detection service and compare results? If another service shows lower rates, it confirms the first service is unreliable.\n\nActually, I think the fundamental issue is clearer than I've been willing to admit. Every version I generate - regardless of approach - is detected at >70% AI probability. The ONLY version that showed lower was v4-1 at 16%, which I've been unable to reproduce.\n\nThe most rational explanation is that v4-1's 16% was a fluke (wrong file, different test, detector glitch), and the TRUE detection rate for ALL my text is 100%.\n\nThis means: **a single LLM cannot generate text that passes this particular AIGC detector.**\n\nThe paid services achieve lower rates because they have infrastructure I don't have. The 15-25% ceiling that aigc-deslop mentioned is accurate.\n\nI should present this conclusion clearly to the user."}
